Let the handler refuse a TCP connection before the stack completes the
handshake.

keqdroid's mihomo on Android rejects connections from apps outside the VPN
(mihomo-uid-owner-guard.patch): such an app can bind a socket to tun0 with no
rights since Linux 5.7. The rule ran only after the forwarder had already
answered the SYN, so the app saw a connection open and then close, which is
proof that the tun answers. A handler that implements TCPRejecter is now asked
first and a refusal gets a reset. A handler without the method sees no change.

On the desktop keqdroid closes IPv6 inside the TUN the same way
(mihomo-v6-tun-reset.patch). There a connection that opened and died made a
browser on IPv6 give up on the site instead of trying IPv4 (complaint of
19.11.2026), and the stack is the one the person picked, so every stack asks:
gVisor in its forwarder, mips before it accepts, and the system stack (system,
and mixed for TCP) before the NAT entry on the first SYN, because the listener
of the OS answers the SYN by itself. The reset is the one the system stack
already sends when it has no NAT port.

The answers come from mihomo-uid-owner-handshake.patch and
mihomo-v6-tun-reset.patch.

Applied to github.com/metacubex/sing-tun at the version mihomo's go.mod pins,
by tool/build_mihomo.ps1, which points mihomo at the patched copy.

